PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core consensus now solidified: shared element = English-specific homophone/near-homophone or phonetic pun supplying the decisive deductive clue (atypical for both authors). This vanishes in translation because target languages lack the exact sound identity. Strong, stable majority support for exactly I (substitute equivalent target-language homophone pair preserving logic), II (audio lets readers hear original English resemblance), and VI (phonetic transcription makes sound relationship explicit). 

Persistent split: phonetic camp (now I-II-VI) vs. cultural-adaptation camp (I-III-VI, led by 1,5,7,9,13,14). Latter claims III removes mismatch at source and V bypasses words; phonetic camp correctly notes III/V alter setting or add visuals without restoring the required auditory identity and risk changing the original deductive structure. IV (accent) and pure V now have negligible support after round-1 shifts. 

Unresolved tension: whether clue is purely auditory (II/VI needed) or can be neutralized by cultural reset. Evidence from round favors phonetic view as minimal sufficient set. No new factual claims about stories themselves; prior private notes on homophone nature remain decisive.}

